  "notes": "The research gives this entry a rule rather than a description, and the rule is inverse: the documented benchmark is 598 syllables in 55 seconds — about 10.9 a second, the density ceiling of the whole compendium — and \"as vocal/lead density rises toward that ceiling, hat and perc density must fall, because the subdivision space is already occupied\". So this model reads as a contradiction until you know that: `hihat.base` is 16th because the research says 16ths are \"necessary to frame the double-time flow\", but `fillDensity` is only 0.3 and `percs.densityPerBar` is 0–1 at −9 dB, both the lowest on this axis. The lead carries the density instead — `melody.densityPerBar` 6–11, the highest here — and sits at register 67–90 because the research says the keys are mid-high \"so the sample sits above the rapid vocal\". The kick takes 16th pickups (`densityPerBar` 3–5, `syncopation` 0.5, `tresilloBias` 0.18) and the roll frequency sits at the top of its band for the \"snare rolls into hooks\". ⛔ The bass is `mirror_kick` at `followRootsProb` 0.93, not `reese_sustain`. The research says \"sustained synth sub… kept simple so the vocal rhythm stays legible\", and the rhythm that literally holds a note emits one per chord and collapses; a near-always-root sub locked to a busy kick is the same record and does not. ⚠ `confidence` is medium and the research marks the producer specifics `[LOW-CONF]`. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are all of the drum numbers: the flow/tempo relationship is documented at record level and The Legendary Traxster's board is not, so `fillDensity` 0.3 is this author's arithmetic on the inverse rule rather than a measurement.",
